# rwv member

## Purpose

Change which repos the active project's `rwv.toml` lists, or how it lists
one: `member add` clones a repo and registers it, `member remove` takes it out,
`member set-role` changes its `role`, `member set-recurse-submodules`
changes whether new checkouts of it populate its submodules, and `member pin`
pins it at a commit you choose.

Each but `member pin` is an **authoring verb**: it mutates the manifest and
regenerates integration-managed content (Cargo workspace, npm workspace, etc.)
so the resulting files can be committed alongside the `rwv.toml` change.
`member add` commits its own change with the member's first pin.

## Invocation

```
rwv member add <url> [--role <role>] [--no-recurse-submodules] [--project <name>] [--no-materialize]
rwv member add <path> --adopt [--role <role>] [--no-recurse-submodules] [--project <name>] [--no-materialize]
rwv member add <creation-address> --new [--param <name>=<value>]... [--params-json <json>] [--role <role>] [--no-recurse-submodules] [--project <name>] [--no-materialize]
rwv member remove <path> [--delete] [--delete-shared-clone] [--project <name>] [--no-materialize]
rwv member set-role <repo> <role> [--project <name>] [--no-materialize]
rwv member set-recurse-submodules <repo> <true|false> [--project <name>] [--no-materialize]
rwv member pin <repo> <rev> [--commit] [--project <name>]
```

Every form takes `--project <name>` to operate on that project rather than the
active one (this does not change `.rwv-active`) and `--no-materialize` to skip
the integration install hooks (`npm install`, `uv sync`, `cargo fetch`, …) of
the regeneration it runs at primary; `rwv materialize` runs them later. Inside
a workweave the hooks are skipped either way.

Run `rwv member --help` for the full clap surface.

## `rwv member add`

### Purpose

Clone a repo and register it in the active project's `rwv.toml`, then
re-run integration activation so ecosystem files (Cargo workspace, npm
workspace, etc.) are updated to include the new member.

#### The commit and the first pin

`add` then commits the entry, the regenerated integration files and
`rwv.lock` in one commit, and records the member's **first pin** in the lock:
the commit it cloned. With `--adopt` the pin is the clone's HEAD where HEAD
sits on the declared branch or that branch's upstream reaches it. A clone
holding commits only it has, and a repo `--new` created with no commits, are
added without a pin and reported as `pin` `none`, with the verbs that give one
(`rwv member pin <repo> <rev> --commit`, `rwv update --remote --repo <repo>
--commit`). Re-running `add` over an entry written by hand gives the member
the pin it lacks, in a commit that carries the entry. The add refuses before
it changes anything over uncommitted changes to tracked files of the project
repo outside `rwv.toml`, `rwv.lock` and the files it regenerates, and when the
project directory is not a repository.

#### Clone placement

Clones always land at the **canonical path** under the primary weave root,
regardless of whether `rwv member add` is invoked from inside a workweave. This
reflects the shared-clone topology: member repos are global infrastructure,
with `git worktree` providing per-workweave isolation. (See
`rwv explain workweave` for the workweave topology model.)

If a clone already exists on disk at the canonical path and one of its remotes
names the same repository, `add` skips the network clone and registers it. A
clone of anything else refuses (`foreign-clone-at-placement`), naming `rwv
member add --adopt <path>` to register it under its own remote. A directory (empty or
not) or file at the placement that is not a clone refuses
(`non-clone-at-placement`); so does one at the workweave's own placement when
`add` or `add --adopt` runs from a workweave.

#### Submodules

`add` gives the canonical clone the member's submodules from upstream, nested
ones included, as `rwv clone` and `rwv update` do (see `rwv explain update`), so
a checkout created later, which never reaches the network, can check them out
from it. A
submodule that cannot be fetched is a warning, not a failure. On re-running
`add` for a member the manifest already names, its `recurse-submodules = false` is
honored; `--adopt` and `--new` fetch nothing, and so does a new member added with
`--no-recurse-submodules`.

#### Workweave-aware worktree materialization

When `rwv member add` is invoked from inside a workweave, it also creates a
`git worktree` for the new repo inside the workweave directory. The
ephemeral branch follows the standard `{project}--{workweave}` naming
convention (matching `rwv workweave create`) — flat, with no third component
derived from the source branch. `add` records an ownership receipt for it, so
`rwv workweave delete` destroys it along with the rest of the workweave's
refs. If the canonical clone has no commits yet (from `--new`), the worktree
creation is skipped; after the first commit, run `rwv member pin <repo> HEAD`
then `rwv doctor --fix` here to materialize it.

#### Adopting an existing clone (`--adopt`)

With `--adopt`, the argument is the path of a clone already under the primary
weave root; a relative path resolves against the directory rwv runs in (or the
`-C`/`-w` directory). The member path recorded is the clone's position under
the weave root containing it. From a workweave, a path inside the workweave
names the member at that position, and the clone registered is the primary's
clone there. `add` reads the URL from the clone's only remote (a clone with
several refuses, `no-remote-url`) and registers it without a network clone. A path that holds no clone refuses
(`no-clone-to-adopt`); a path under no weave root refuses
(`adopt-path-outside-weave`); a workweave path holding a clone that is not a
worktree of the primary's refuses (`foreign-clone-in-workweave`).

#### Spelling the argument

The argument's meaning comes from how it is spelled. A URL — `https://`,
`git@`, or `file://` for a local remote — or registry shorthand such as
`github/owner/repo` is cloned from. An existing clone is registered only under `--adopt`.
Anything else reads as a path, which could mean either, so `add` refuses
(`unresolvable-repo-source`) and names both spellings: `rwv member add
file://<absolute path>` to clone from a local remote, and `rwv member add --adopt
<path>` to register the clone at that path.

#### Creation (`--new`)

`--new` is expected to be rare. The common path is to create the repo with
the registry's own tools (a forge's web UI, `gh`/`glab`, or `mkdir` + `git
init` for a local one) and then run a plain `rwv member add <url>`, which already
works for anything `placement` covers. `--new` is a secondary convenience for
when that isn't done yet.

With `--new`, the positional argument is a **creation address**, not a URL or
a path — a bare registry name (`local`), or a three-segment shorthand
(`github/acme/fresh`) that fills the `owner` and `repo` parameters for
whichever registry the first segment names. Any parameter the registry still
needs — every parameter, for a bare registry name — is supplied with
`--param <name>=<value>` (repeatable) or as one JSON object via
`--params-json '{"name": "value"}'`. A parameter named in more than one of
these three places refuses rather than picking a winner.

Creation is registry-dispatched: each registry declares its own parameters
and plans what gets created from them.

{{MSG:creation_params_table}}

- **A domain registry** (`github`, `gitlab`, `bitbucket`) needs `owner` and
  `repo`. Nothing is created remotely — rwv has no HTTP client — so the
  member is `git init`'d locally and its remote is set to the minted URL as
  an intent. `rwv member add github/acme/fresh --new` and `rwv member add github --new
  --param owner=acme --param repo=fresh` are equivalent.
- **`local`** needs `root`, `owner`, and `repo`. Creation is two-sided: rwv
  creates a **bare** repository at `<root>/<owner>/<repo>` and clones the
  member from it, so the member's remote is real from birth and `git push
  -u` works immediately. `root` must already exist (rwv creates
  `<root>/<owner>/<repo>`, not `<root>` itself) and must be outside the
  weave. A relative `root` resolves against the directory rwv runs in — the
  `-C` or `-w` directory when one is given — and the manifest records the
  absolute path it names. `rwv member add local/acme/fresh --new` fills `owner` and `repo` from the
  shorthand and still needs `--param root=<dir>`.

The placement path is always derived from the minted URL, the same way a
plain `rwv member add <url>` derives it — the creation address only selects a
registry and supplies parameters, never a layout.

### Arguments

- `<url>` — repository URL or shorthand. Parsed through the registry
  topology to derive the canonical local path. Accepts `https://`, `git@`,
  `file://`, or registry shorthand such as `github/owner/repo`.
- `--adopt` — the argument is the path of an existing clone under the primary
  weave root, registered without cloning (see "Adopting an existing clone").
- `--role <role>` — role for the repo in the manifest (`owned`, `fork`,
  `dependency`, `reference`). Defaults to `owned`.
- `--no-recurse-submodules` — record `recurse-submodules = false` for the new
  member, so new checkouts of it leave its submodules uninitialized. A member
  the manifest already lists keeps its entry as it is; `rwv member
  set-recurse-submodules` changes it.
- `--new` — create a new repo instead of cloning. The positional argument
  becomes a creation address (see "Creation" above) rather than a URL or path.
- `--param <name>=<value>` — a creation parameter (repeatable). Requires
  `--new`. See the table above for what each registry declares.
- `--params-json <json>` — the same parameters as one JSON object of string
  values. Requires `--new`.

### Output

Progress messages to stderr:

- `Added '<repo-path>' to manifest` — repo was registered.
- `Pinned '<repo-path>' at <commit>` — the commit that went into the lock as
  its first pin; without one, the `pin` `none` report in its place.
- `Repository already exists in manifest at '<repo-path>'; re-running its
  activation` — the manifest is left as it is, and activation runs again, so a
  weave an earlier run left short (a surfaced link gone missing, install hooks
  that failed) is repaired by repeating the same command. An entry written into
  `rwv.toml` by hand is finished the same way: when its canonical clone is not
  on disk, `add` clones it from the URL the manifest records, creates the
  workweave's worktree when run in one, then regenerates. The URL given must
  name the same repo as the manifest's entry, or the add refuses
  (`occupied-placement`).
- `Directory already exists at '<path>', skipping clone` — clone present,
  not re-cloned.

Integration activation output (symlinks created/removed) goes to stderr.

A side effect the verb skipped is said on stderr in one line naming the
command that performs it; nothing is said when it skipped none:

- with `--no-materialize`: `[skipped] core: the install hooks did not run
  (--no-materialize); `rwv materialize` runs them`;
- inside a workweave, where an authoring verb runs no hooks: `[skipped] core:
  the install hooks did not run (an authoring verb runs none inside a
  workweave); `rwv materialize` runs them`;
- for a project the weave root does not present (`--project`), whose files it
  regenerates and stops there: `[skipped] core: the weave-root links were not
  refreshed (`<project>` is not the project this weave root presents); `rwv
  activate <project>` refreshes them`, and the same line for the install hooks,
  ending `runs them`;
- for a hook whose tool is not on `PATH`: its `tool-missing` warning.

### Exit codes

- `0` — repo added (or already present), manifest updated, activation
  succeeded.
- non-zero — URL could not be resolved, clone failed, manifest could not be
  written, or activation returned an error.

The manifest edit and the regeneration it drives land together or not at all.
When activation refuses before the install hooks run — an integration rejects
the new member, or a surfacing path is occupied — `rwv.toml` and every
regenerated file are put back as they were, and the error says so. The
install hooks run after that point and have no undo, so when one fails the
error says `rwv.toml` was updated; re-running the add re-runs them. A failed
hook is not a refused add: the member stands.

A refused add accounts for what it had created by then. A clone it fetched,
and a workweave worktree it made, stay on disk: the error names each one and
says that once the cause is fixed, re-running the same add reuses it, or
`rwv member remove <path> --delete` removes it instead (in the workweave for its
worktree, then in the primary weave for the clone). Fix the cause first either
way: the remove regenerates the project's files too, so it refuses for the same
reason while the cause stands. What
`--new` created — the bare upstream under `--param root`, the new member, and
any directory made to hold them — holds nothing but what the add put there,
so the refused add removes it and says so.

### Examples

Add an owned repo (clone + register):

```
rwv member add https://github.com/myorg/myrepo
```

Add a dependency (read-only; won't be pushed or edited):

```
rwv member add https://github.com/third-party/lib --role dependency
```

Register a repo that's already cloned:

```
rwv member add --adopt github/myorg/existing-repo
```

Clone from a local bare repository:

```
rwv member add file:///srv/repos/myorg/lib.git
```

Create a repo at a registry that hosts it (member `git init`'d locally,
remote set to the minted URL as an intent):

```
rwv member add github/myorg/new-project --new
```

Create a repo with no registry backing it, from scratch — rwv creates a bare
upstream under `--param root` and clones the member from it:

```
rwv member add local --new --param root=/srv/repos --param owner=myorg --param repo=new-project
```

The same creation, scripted:

```
rwv member add local --new --params-json '{"root": "/srv/repos", "owner": "myorg", "repo": "new-project"}'
```

### Common errors

- *unrecognized URL* — the URL does not parse through any built-in registry
  and has no derivable local path. Check the URL format.
- *not a URL and no registry reads it as shorthand*
  (`unresolvable-repo-source`) — the argument reads as a path. Spell a local remote as `file://<absolute path>`, or pass
  `--adopt` to register the clone at that path.
- *no clone to adopt* (`no-clone-to-adopt`) — `--adopt` names a path with no
  clone.
- *adopt path outside weave* (`adopt-path-outside-weave`) — `--adopt` names a
  location under neither the primary weave root nor the current workweave.
  A relative path resolves against the directory rwv runs in, and a symlink in
  it is followed.
- *foreign clone in workweave* (`foreign-clone-in-workweave`) — `--adopt`
  names a clone inside the workweave that is not a worktree of the primary's
  clone at the same path.
- *non-clone at placement* (`non-clone-at-placement`) — the placement, or
  from a workweave the workweave's own placement, holds a directory or file
  that is not a clone. Move it aside and re-run.
- *foreign clone at placement* (`foreign-clone-at-placement`) — the URL's
  placement already holds a clone of a different repository; register it with
  `--adopt`, or move it aside.
- *clone failed* — network error or authentication problem; inspect git output.
- *manifest parse failure* — `rwv.toml` could not be loaded; verify the file
  is valid TOML.
- *missing creation parameter* — `--new` and a required parameter (see the
  table above) was not supplied by the address, `--param`, or `--params-json`.
- *duplicate creation parameter* — the same parameter name arrived through
  more than one of the address shorthand, `--param`, and `--params-json`.
- *occupied placement* — the URL's or creation address's placement already
  maps to a different URL in the manifest.

## `rwv member remove`

### Purpose

Remove a repo from the active project's `rwv.toml` and re-run integration
activation so ecosystem files (Cargo workspace, npm workspace, etc.) are
updated to no longer include the removed member.

Removing from the manifest does **not** delete the checkout from disk by
default. Use `--delete` to also remove it.

#### Finishing a hand edit

An entry already removed from `rwv.toml` by hand is finished by the same
command. When the path is no longer in the manifest but this workspace still
holds a checkout there that is this project's — this project's `rwv.lock`
still names it, or no other project lists it — `remove` regenerates the
integration files without it and says it was already out of the manifest.
`--delete` then deletes that checkout exactly as it would have deleted a
listed one. A path with no such checkout refuses (`repo-not-in-manifest`).

#### What `--delete` deletes

`--delete` removes the checkout in the workspace the command runs in:

- **In the primary weave**, the canonical clone at its path under the weave
  root.
- **In a workweave**, that workweave's worktree of the repo, and the branch
  the workweave was given in the canonical clone, so nothing of the
  workweave's still claims that clone. A branch holding commits that neither
  the workweave's parent nor the primary weave holds stays, and a warning
  names it. The canonical clone in the primary is never touched from a
  workweave; it is removed when the change lands and the primary drops the
  repo, or by `rwv member remove <path> --delete` in the primary weave.

Every guard runs before the manifest is written, so a refused delete leaves
`rwv.toml` and the checkout as they were:

- in the primary, a clone **another project's `rwv.toml` lists** refuses
  (`shared-clone-referenced`) unless `--delete-shared-clone` is given;
- the checkout's *uncommitted changes*, and in the primary every **live
  checkout derived from the clone** — a workweave's, or a worktree added by
  hand — are judged together and refuse as one: one line per state, on the
  `edits` and `checkout` kinds, with the choices each state prints;
- *commits no other clone reaches* refuse
  (`dropped-repo-has-unique-commits`): in the primary, any branch with no
  remote counterpart or with commits ahead of it, and any backup that holds
  commits no branch reaches (`rwv backup list` shows them; `rwv backup drop
  <repo>` drops the ones that are not wanted); in a workweave, a worktree
  tip the canonical clone does not contain;
- in the primary, a clone rwv still holds **ownership receipts** for refuses
  (`store-still-claimed`).

(See `rwv explain workweave` for the workweave topology model.)

### Arguments

- `<path>` — manifest-relative path of the repo to remove (as listed in
  `rwv.toml`, e.g. `github/myorg/myrepo`).
- `--delete` — also remove this workspace's checkout from disk (see "What
  `--delete` deletes" above).
- `--delete-shared-clone` — delete the clone even when another project still
  references it. Requires `--delete`.

### Output

Progress messages to stderr:

- `Removed '<path>' from manifest`, or `'<path>' was already out of the
  manifest; regenerated without it` for an entry removed by hand.
- Integration activation output (symlinks created/removed).
- `Deleted '<checkout>'` when `--delete` removed one.

A side effect the verb skipped is said on stderr in one line naming the
command that performs it; nothing is said when it skipped none:

- with `--no-materialize`: `[skipped] core: the install hooks did not run
  (--no-materialize); `rwv materialize` runs them`;
- inside a workweave, where an authoring verb runs no hooks: `[skipped] core:
  the install hooks did not run (an authoring verb runs none inside a
  workweave); `rwv materialize` runs them`;
- for a project the weave root does not present (`--project`), whose files it
  regenerates and stops there: `[skipped] core: the weave-root links were not
  refreshed (`<project>` is not the project this weave root presents); `rwv
  activate <project>` refreshes them`, and the same line for the install hooks,
  ending `runs them`;
- for a hook whose tool is not on `PATH`: its `tool-missing` warning.

### Exit codes

- `0` — repo removed from manifest (or already out of it), activation
  succeeded.
- non-zero — no entry and no leftover checkout at the path, a `--delete`
  guard refused, directory removal failed, or activation returned an error.

The manifest edit and the regeneration it drives land together or not at all.
When activation refuses before the install hooks run, `rwv.toml` and every
regenerated file are put back as they were, the error says so, and `--delete`
deletes nothing. The install hooks run after that point and have no undo, so
when one fails the error says `rwv.toml` was updated.

### Examples

Remove from manifest (keep clone on disk):

```
rwv member remove github/myorg/old-repo
```

Remove from manifest and delete this workspace's checkout:

```
rwv member remove github/myorg/old-repo --delete
```

Remove and delete the clone even if another project references it:

```
rwv member remove github/myorg/old-repo --delete --delete-shared-clone
```

### Common errors

- *path not in manifest* — the given path does not appear in `rwv.toml` and no
  leftover checkout of this project's is there. Check the exact manifest path
  with `rwv status`.
- *uncommitted changes* or *unique commits* — `--delete` would lose work,
  a backup's included. Commit and push it, or discard it (`rwv backup drop`
  for a backup), then re-run.
- *another project references this path; refusing --delete without
  --delete-shared-clone* — the clone is shared. Verify the other project and
  use `--delete-shared-clone` only when safe.
- *manifest parse failure* — `rwv.toml` could not be loaded; verify the file
  is valid TOML.

## `rwv member set-role`

### Purpose

Change a repo's `role` in this workspace's `rwv.toml`, regenerate the
integration-managed files from the edited manifest, and settle the repo's
files for its new role.

The regeneration matters because a
`reference` repo is excluded from every ecosystem's build graph, so making a
repo a reference removes it from a generated Cargo workspace, npm workspace
or `go.work`, and giving it back another role puts it back.

Settling is what makes the role true on disk:

- **To `reference`:** every tracked regular file of the repo loses its write
  bits. Directories keep theirs, so untracked files can still be created and
  the VCS can still replace files on an update. Tracked symlinks are never
  chmod'ed.
- **From `reference`:** the owner's write bit comes back. A file that shares
  its storage with another workspace's copy is first replaced by a private
  copy, because write bits belong to the file rather than to the name.

The change is local to this workspace until it lands. `rwv sync-to` carries
it to the target, which settles the repo on landing, and every other
workspace settles it on its next `rwv sync`. A hand edit of `role` in
`rwv.toml` converges the same way, and `rwv doctor --fix` settles it in
place (`rwv explain member-permissions`).

### Arguments

- `<repo>` — the repo's manifest-relative path, as `rwv.toml` lists it (e.g.
  `github/myorg/myrepo`).
- `<role>` — `owned`, `fork`, `dependency` or `reference`. Any other value is
  rejected before anything is read.

### Output

Progress messages go to stderr: the integration activation output, then the
change made. When the repo already has the role, `role` leaves `rwv.toml`
alone, regenerates from it, settles the member's files for that role (so it
finishes a hand edit of the role), and reports how many files it settled.

A side effect the verb skipped is said on stderr in one line naming the
command that performs it; nothing is said when it skipped none:

- with `--no-materialize`: `[skipped] core: the install hooks did not run
  (--no-materialize); `rwv materialize` runs them`;
- inside a workweave, where an authoring verb runs no hooks: `[skipped] core:
  the install hooks did not run (an authoring verb runs none inside a
  workweave); `rwv materialize` runs them`;
- for a project the weave root does not present (`--project`), whose files it
  regenerates and stops there: `[skipped] core: the weave-root links were not
  refreshed (`<project>` is not the project this weave root presents); `rwv
  activate <project>` refreshes them`, and the same line for the install hooks,
  ending `runs them`;
- for a hook whose tool is not on `PATH`: its `tool-missing` warning.

### Exit codes

- `0` — the role changed (or already was the one asked for).
- non-zero — the path is not in the manifest; the change is to `reference`
  while the repo has uncommitted changes to tracked files (commit, stash or
  discard them first); or the regeneration failed.

The manifest edit and the regeneration land together or not at all: when
activation refuses before the install hooks run, `rwv.toml` and every
regenerated file are put back as they were, and the repo's files keep the
permissions of the role they had. The install hooks run after that point and
have no undo, so when one fails the error says `rwv.toml` was updated, and the
repo is settled for its new role regardless.

### Examples

Keep a repo around as read-only study material:

```
rwv member set-role github/myorg/vendored-lib reference
```

Start working on it again:

```
rwv member set-role github/myorg/vendored-lib owned
```

## `rwv member set-recurse-submodules`

### Purpose

Set a repo's `recurse-submodules` in this workspace's `rwv.toml`, and regenerate
the integration-managed files from the edited manifest. The setting says whether
a new checkout of the member populates the submodules it records, from the
module data the canonical clone already holds and never from the network;
`false` leaves them uninitialized. A checkout that already exists keeps the
submodules it has. The key is written only when it is `false`: `true` is the
default and is not written out.

The change is local to this workspace until it lands, as `member set-role`'s
is. A hand edit of `recurse-submodules` in `rwv.toml` converges the same way.

### Arguments

- `<repo>` — the repo's manifest-relative path, as `rwv.toml` lists it (e.g.
  `github/myorg/myrepo`).
- `<true|false>` — the setting to record. Any other value is rejected before
  anything is read.

### Output

Progress messages go to stderr: the integration activation output, then the
change made. When the repo already has the setting, the verb leaves `rwv.toml`
alone, regenerates from it, and says nothing changed.

### Exit codes

- `0` — the setting changed (or already was the one asked for).
- non-zero — the path is not in the manifest, or the regeneration failed.

The manifest edit and the regeneration land together or not at all, as for
`member set-role`.

### Examples

Leave a repo's submodules out of new checkouts:

```
rwv member set-recurse-submodules github/myorg/vendored-lib false
```

Populate them again:

```
rwv member set-recurse-submodules github/myorg/vendored-lib true
```

## `rwv member pin`

### Purpose

Pin one member at a commit of your choosing, in either direction, and align
the member to it. It is the verb that gives a member its first pin when
`member add` could not, and the one that moves a pin backward.

### Arguments

- `<repo>` — manifest-relative path of the member (as listed in `rwv.toml`).
- `<rev>` — a commit id, or a name that resolves to one in the member's clone.
  Where the member follows a declared branch (every member of a declared
  workspace; a `dependency` or `reference` member of a feature), the branch or
  its upstream must reach the commit, or the verb refuses with `pin` `not on
  declared branch`. A name the clone does not resolve refuses with `name`
  `no-such-branch`.
- `--commit` — commit `rwv.lock` after writing it, together with `rwv.toml`
  when it has uncommitted changes. Other uncommitted changes in the project
  repo refuse.

### What it does

Every refusal is reported before the lock is written or the member moves.
Then, in order:

1. A member left detached with commits no branch holds is backed up first.
2. `rwv.lock` takes the pin; the verb says what it replaced, and reports
   `re-pinned` when the new commit does not descend from the old pin.
3. The member is aligned to the pin as `rwv update` aligns it. A `<rev>` behind
   the tip of the branch the member is on leaves the checkout at that tip, and
   the verb reports where it stands against the pin: `pin` `ahead`, or `unpinned`
   when nothing else holds the commits beyond it. The next `rwv lock` records
   the tip again.
4. With `--commit`, the lock is committed.

A workspace that holds no checkout of the member gets the pin only; `rwv
update` creates the checkout at it. A detached workspace refuses.

### Exit codes

- `0` — the pin was written (and committed with `--commit`).
- non-zero — the member is not in the manifest, `<rev>` does not resolve or is
  not on the declared branch, the member cannot move (uncommitted changes, an
  operation in progress), or the commit refused.

### Examples

Give a member the first pin it lacks, at its checkout:

```
rwv member pin github/myorg/lib HEAD --commit
```

Move a pin backward, by name:

```
rwv member pin github/myorg/lib v1.4.0
```
